---
import '../../styles/global.css';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>The Advanced Photo Editing Masterclass - Ivan Chan</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Learn to think like an expert photo editor and create clean, impactful images with intention."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="scroll-progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <nav>
      <div class="wrap nav-inner">
        <div class="brand">IVAN <span>Chan</span></div>
        <div class="nav-links">
          <a href="#framework">The Framework</a>
          <a href="#curriculum">Curriculum</a>
          <a href="#bonuses">Bonuses</a>
          <a class="btn btn-dark" href="#enroll">Join the waitlist</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <header class="hero">
      <div class="hero-bg">
        <img src="/assets/hero-background.jpg" alt="Dramatic mountain sunrise photographed by Ivan Chan" />
      </div>
      <div class="wrap hero-content">
        <div class="eyebrow">The Advanced Photo Editing Masterclass</div>
        <h1 class="display">Stop moving sliders.<br /><em>Start seeing.</em></h1>
        <p>
          Learn the principles behind clean, powerful photo edits-so you can diagnose any image, choose the right tools,
          and make it look the way you actually imagined.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="btn btn-dark" href="#enroll">Join the waitlist ↗</a>
          <a class="btn btn-light" href="#framework">See how it works</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="proof-strip" data-reveal>
      <div class="wrap proof-row">
        <span>Built for intermediate photographers</span>
        <span>Lightroom-first workflow</span>
        <span>Real RAW exercises</span>
        <span>Principles over presets</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="section" data-reveal>
      <div class="wrap intro-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">The gap nobody talks about</div>
          <h2 class="display">You know Lightroom.<br />But you know your edits could be <span class="marker">better.</span></h2>
        </div>
        <div class="intro-copy">
          <p>
            You’ve watched plenty of Youtube tutorials. You understand what most of the sliders and tools do. But when a new RAW file
            opens, you still find yourself experimenting until something looks “<span class="marker">good enough</span>.”
          </p>
          <p>
            The problem isn’t that you need another list of slider settings. You need a stronger understanding of 
            <strong><span class="marker"> what makes an image work visually</span></strong> - and a repeatable way to decide what to change, where to
            change it, and why.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="wrap problem-header">
        <div class="eyebrow">WHAT I SEE MOST INTERMEDIATE EDITORS STRUGGLE WITH</div>
      </div>
      <div class="wrap problem-cards" data-stagger>
        <article class="card">
          <div class="card-num">01</div>
          <h3>Knowing what to change</h3>
          <p>Tutorials often show what someone did without teaching the core visual principles of color, contrast, and attention behind it. So you never know when to apply it in your own images outside of that specific scenario.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <div class="card-num">02</div>
          <h3>Knowing what tools to reach for</h3>
          <p>Many tools in Lightroom are powerful, but they can introduce unwanted side effects that go unnoticed. The compounding of these effects is what causes an image to fall apart over time. This is where a deep understanding of the trade-offs of different tools becomes important.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <div class="card-num">03</div>
          <h3>Knowing how to make elements work in harmony</h3>
          <p>Advanced editing is less about using increasingly complex techniques and more about how to use color, saturation, and luminance to make elements work in harmony.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="wrap bridge-copy" data-reveal>
        <h3>This is what I’m here to help you with.</h3>
        <p>
          The goal isn’t to memorize more tools. It’s to build a <span class="marker">way of seeing, diagnosing, and editing</span> that you can bring to any photo.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section dark chess-section" id="framework" data-reveal>
      <div class="king-stage" aria-hidden="true">
        <canvas id="chess-king-canvas"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="framework-head">
          <div>
            <div class="eyebrow">Your repeatable editing system</div>
            <h2 class="display">Play C.H.E.S.S.</h2>
          </div>
          <p class="muted">A practical framework that turns a vague creative process into a clear sequence of decisions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="chess" data-stagger>
          <article class="chess-card">
            <div class="chess-letter">C</div>
            <div><h3>Clean</h3><p>Remove distractions, recover detail, and build a strong visual foundation.</p></div>
          </article>
          <article class="chess-card">
            <div class="chess-letter">H</div>
            <div><h3>Harmonize</h3><p>Simplify colour and tonal relationships so the image feels cohesive and easy to digest.</p></div>
          </article>
          <article class="chess-card">
            <div class="chess-letter">E</div>
            <div><h3>Enhance</h3><p>Shape attention, visual flow, contrast, depth, and subject separation with intention.</p></div>
          </article>
          <article class="chess-card">
            <div class="chess-letter">S</div>
            <div><h3>Stylize</h3><p>Build mood through creative light, colour casts, and a deliberate visual identity.</p></div>
          </article>
          <article class="chess-card">
            <div class="chess-letter">S</div>
            <div><h3>Step Back</h3><p>Reset your eyes, evaluate the whole image, and refine without overediting.</p></div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="split" data-reveal="scale">
      <div class="split-image">
        <img src="/assets/editor-mindset-section.jpg" alt="Aerial river landscape photographed by Ivan Chan" />
      </div>
      <div class="split-copy">
        <div class="eyebrow">The real skill</div>
        <h2 class="display">Learn to <span class="marker">think</span> like an editor.</h2>
        <p>
          I’m not here to teach you how your photos should look or to simply recreate my style (although you’ll be able
          to by the end). 
        </p>
        <p>
        I’m here to teach you the principles behind visual aesthetics—<span class="accent-words">brightness,
          color, contrast, texture, and visual flow</span>—so you can make <span class="marker">your</span> style look
          beautiful.
        </p>
        <p>
          Think of it as learning how to fish instead of relying on someone else to keep giving you fish.
        </p>
        <p>
          Today’s editing tools evolve rapidly. It feels like Lightroom releases new features every month. But the
          <span class="marker"> principles behind what makes an image look good</span> don’t change.
        </p>
        <div class="quote">
          “Software changes. The <span class="marker">ability to see what an image needs</span> does not.”
        </div>
        <p>
          Once you understand those principles, you’ll be able to evaluate any new editing tool—its strengths, its
          weaknesses, and when to use it—without waiting for another tutorial to tell you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section curriculum" id="curriculum" data-reveal>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="masterclass-head">
          <figure class="masterclass-pages" aria-hidden="true">
            <img src="/assets/course-editing-cropped.png" alt="" />
          </figure>
          <h2 class="display">Inside the Masterclass</h2>
          <p class="curriculum-subtitle">From “something feels off” to knowing exactly why.</p>
          <div class="curriculum-pills" aria-label="Course details" data-stagger>
            <span>29 lessons</span>
            <span>7 modules</span>
            <span>Text-based Theory with Visual Examples</span>
            <span>Video Walkthroughs</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="modules" data-stagger>
          <div class="module"><div class="module-index">01</div><h3>Orientation & the Editing Framework</h3><p>Build the mindset, workflow, and C.H.E.S.S. structure that organizes the entire editing process.</p></div>
          <div class="module"><div class="module-index">02</div><h3>Seeing Like an Editor</h3><p>Understand visual attention, weight, balance, flow, hierarchy, and how the eye moves through an image.</p></div>
          <div class="module"><div class="module-index">03</div><h3>Selections & Picking the Right Tools</h3><p>Choose tools based on precision, speed, power, risk, roll-off, and the exact pixels you need to affect.</p></div>
          <div class="module"><div class="module-index">04</div><h3>Clean & Harmonize</h3><p>Remove distractions, protect detail, correct casts, and create cleaner relationships within and between elements.</p></div>
          <div class="module"><div class="module-index">05</div><h3>Enhance</h3><p>Create visual impact through tonal and colour contrast, saturation, depth cues, sharpness, framing, and separation.</p></div>
          <div class="module"><div class="module-index">06</div><h3>Stylize</h3><p>Shape mood and identity using creative light, colour, atmosphere, and intentional departures from reality.</p></div>
          <div class="module"><div class="module-index">07</div><h3>Full Edit Walkthroughs</h3><p>Watch the entire decision-making process unfold across different kinds of challenging RAW files.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="course-preview" data-reveal>
          <figure class="course-preview-screen">
            <img src="/assets/course-editing-walkthrough.png" alt="Screenshot of a video walkthrough lesson inside the course" />
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="exercise-feature" data-reveal>
          <div class="exercise-copy">
            <h3><span class="marker">20 practical exercises</span> with included RAWs</h3>
            <p>
              Designed to isolate particular stages of the edit so you can practice applying specific theories and
              techniques. This is the best way to <span class="marker">lock in the new learnings</span>, and is what
              most photography courses miss.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="exercise-stack" aria-label="Course exercise screenshots">
            <figure class="exercise-card exercise-card-back">
              <img src="/assets/course-exercises.png" alt="Screenshot of a practical course exercise" />
            </figure>
            <figure class="exercise-card exercise-card-front">
              <img src="/assets/course-exercise-list.png" alt="Screenshot of the course exercise list" />
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="scroll-edit dark" data-scroll-edit style="--edit-progress: 0">
      <div class="scroll-edit-sticky">
        <div class="wrap scroll-edit-inner">
          <h2 class="display scroll-edit-title">
            <span>Learn how to take images</span>
            <span class="scroll-edit-word" aria-live="polite">
              <span>from this</span>
              <span>to this</span>
            </span>
          </h2>
          <figure class="scroll-edit-frame" aria-label="Raw to edited image comparison">
            <img class="scroll-edit-img scroll-edit-raw" src="/assets/course-huemul-raw.jpg" alt="RAW mountain landscape before editing" />
            <img class="scroll-edit-img scroll-edit-final" src="/assets/course-huemul-edited.jpg" alt="Fully edited mountain landscape after editing" />
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>


    <section class="image-gallery dark" data-image-gallery aria-label="Course image gallery">
      <div class="image-gallery-track" data-gallery-track>
        <div class="image-gallery-set">
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/China.jpg" alt="Mountain landscape in China" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Iceland.jpg" alt="Iceland landscape photograph" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Philippines-2.jpg" alt="Philippines coastal landscape" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Washington.jpg" alt="Washington mountain landscape" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Washington-2.jpg" alt="Washington forest and mountain scene" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Patagonia.jpg" alt="Patagonia mountain landscape" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Philippines.jpg" alt="Philippines island landscape" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Vietnam.jpg" alt="Vietnam landscape photograph" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Indonesia.jpg" alt="Indonesia landscape photograph" draggable="false" /></figure>
        </div>
        <div class="image-gallery-set" aria-hidden="true">
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/China.jpg" alt="" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Iceland.jpg" alt="" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Philippines-2.jpg" alt="" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Washington.jpg" alt="" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Washington-2.jpg" alt="" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Patagonia.jpg" alt="" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Philippines.jpg" alt="" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Vietnam.jpg" alt="" draggable="false" /></figure>
          <figure class="image-gallery-item"><img src="/assets/course-gallery/Indonesia.jpg" alt="" draggable="false" /></figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section bonus" id="bonuses" data-reveal>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="eyebrow">The Editor’s Toolkit</div>
        <h2 class="display">Resources you’ll keep using<br />after the course ends.</h2>
        <div class="bonus-grid" data-stagger>
          <article class="bonus-card"><div class="bonus-icon">✓</div><div><h3>C.H.E.S.S. Editing Checklist</h3><p>A printable, repeatable workflow to keep beside Lightroom until the process becomes second nature.</p></div></article>
          <article class="bonus-card"><div class="bonus-icon">RAW</div><div><h3>RAW Practice Library</h3><p>Work through increasingly difficult files and compare your choices against completed reference edits.</p></div></article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section community" data-reveal>
      <div class="wrap community-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">Continue improving after the course</div>
          <h2 class="display">A community built around better feedback.</h2>
          <p>Submit your edits, learn from selected critiques, and practice articulating why an image works-not just whether you like it.</p>
          <ul class="tick-list">
            <li>Recurring live editing walkthroughs & group critique sessions</li>
            <li>Structured peer feedback using C.H.E.S.S.</li>
            <li>Editing challenges and practice prompts</li>
            <li>A growing archive of real-world critiques</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="community-photo">
          <img src="/assets/community.jpg" alt="Community of photographers learning together" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="price" id="enroll" data-reveal="scale">
      <div class="wrap price-card">
        <div class="price-copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">Founding access</div>
          <h2 class="display">Become the editor your camera deserves.</h2>
          <p>Join the early-access list to be first to hear when enrollment opens, receive launch pricing, and get a behind-the-scenes look at the course.</p>
          <p class="small">Replace the placeholder form action and pricing below once your Kit form and offer are finalized.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="price-box">
          <div class="eyebrow">Launch price placeholder</div>
          <div class="price-amount">$299</div>
          <p>Lifetime course access + practice files + digital bonuses + introductory community access.</p>
          <a class="btn" style="background:var(--lime);color:#111;margin-top:18px" href="#">Join the waitlist ↗</a>
          <p class="small">No spam. Just course updates and useful editing education.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section faq" data-reveal>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="eyebrow">Questions</div>
        <h2 class="display">Before you join.</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open><summary>Who is this course for?</summary><p>Intermediate photographers who already understand the main Lightroom tools but want to develop stronger visual judgment, cleaner technique, and a more intentional workflow.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is this a beginner Lightroom course?</summary><p>No. The course includes tool references where useful, but it focuses on advanced decision-making rather than explaining every slider from scratch.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Will this teach me Ivan’s exact style?</summary><p>You will see how Ivan creates his own images, but the goal is not to clone a single look. The principles are designed to help you build whatever style feels true to your work.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Do I need Lightroom Classic?</summary><p>The exercises are designed around Lightroom workflows. Final platform requirements and any Lightroom-versus-Lightroom-Classic differences should be confirmed before launch.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Will I receive feedback on my own images?</summary><p>The paid community can include selected live critiques, structured critique threads, peer feedback, and limited personalized review without promising unlimited one-to-one access.</p></details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="final-cta" data-reveal="scale">
      <img src="/assets/final-cta-background.jpg" alt="Adventure photographer on a glacier" />
      <div class="final-inner">
        <div class="eyebrow">Your next level is not another preset</div>
        <h2 class="display">It’s learning how to see.</h2>
        <p>Build the judgment, process, and confidence to open any RAW file and know where to begin.</p>
        <a class="btn" style="background:var(--lime);color:#111;margin-top:18px" href="#enroll">Get early access ↗</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer>
      <div class="wrap footer-row">
        <span>© 2026 Ivan Chan Photography</span>
        <span>Advanced Photo Editing Masterclass</span>
      </div>
    </footer>

    <script src="../../scripts/landing-page.js"></script>
    <script src="../../scripts/chess-king.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
